    deepExplanation: "Context propagation follows the React tree. A provider establishes a value for its subtree, and consumers that read that context can react when the provider value changes. The important distinction is that Context removes prop-drilling; it does not make the value global, nor does it automatically prevent consumer updates. Provider boundaries also determine which value a consumer receives.\\n\\nExample:\\n\\n```tsx\\nimport { createContext, useContext } from \"react\";\n\ntype Theme = \"light\" | \"dark\";\nconst ThemeContext = createContext<Theme>(\"light\");\n\nfunction ThemeButton() {\n  const theme = useContext(ThemeContext);\n  return <button data-theme={theme}>Save</button>;\n}\n\nexport function App() {\n  return (\n    <ThemeContext.Provider value=\"dark\">\n      <Toolbar>\n        <ThemeButton />\n      </Toolbar>\n    </ThemeContext.Provider>\n  );\n}\\n```",
